.SH DESCRIPTION
These commands read an argument and decide what follows from it.
A language model writes a document's argument down as a logic file;
.I prove
decides, by rule, whether its conclusion follows, and says what is
wrong where it does not: the case that refutes it, the fallacy's name,
the premise missing, the premise never stated, the objection left
unanswered.
The model reads; the rules decide.
Each command writes one finding to a line as
.IB file : line :
.IB rule :
.IR message ,
the form of
.IR wwb (1),
so that Xenith and
.IR acme (1)
open it with button 3 and
.IR wwb 's
.IR gate ,
.I delta
and
.I tally
read it.
.PP
.I Philns
puts the commands in the name space as
.BI /dis/phil/ name ,
run as
.BI phil/ name ,
and the same directory as
.BR /dis/\(*fwb ;
its prompts as
.BR /lib/phil ;
and this manual as
.BR /man/1/phil .
The workbench reads prose through the Writer's Workbench: its sentence
numbering
.RI ( wwb/sentences ),
its prose library, and its way to a model
.RI ( wwb/ask ,
and
.B $wwbmodel
to choose one).
If
.B /dis/wwb
is not in the name space,
.I philns
runs the
.I wwbns
of
.IR wwbdir ,
by default
.B infernode-wwb
beside
.IR dir .
.PP
What the commands can and cannot do for a philosophical problem is in
.BR docs/REACH.md ;
the logic file is described in
.BR docs/ARGUMENT-FORMAT.md .
.TP
.B phil/argue
Has a language model write a document's argument as a logic file,
given the sentences numbered and the instructions in
.BR /lib/phil/argue ,
then runs
.I prove
over it and the document.
Only lines in the logic file's form are kept from the answer.
A file the prover cannot read (a formula that does not parse, a sentence
the document does not have, no conclusion) goes back to the model with
the prover's complaints, twice at most, before it is proved.
The model is told to write steps, objections and replies, and the logic
.RB ( s5 ,
.BR kd )
where the text calls for them.
With
.B -s
.IR mine ,
the writer's own logic file for the document, the model reads the text
cold, given only that file's atoms, and
.I prove -c
sets its reading beside the writer's, sentence by sentence: where they
differ is where the prose does not say what its writer meant.
Write the argument first, then the prose, then this.
With
.BR -k ,
the model reads the text that many times, each afresh but with the first
reading's atoms, and
.I prove -c
compares the readings.
Each question it asks about a sentence read two ways is put to the model
afresh, to answer yes or no
.RB ( -q :
left in the output for a person to answer, with
.IR "prove -c -a" );
of the readings the answers leave, the one with fewest mismatches with
its sentences (below), and of those the one most of the others agree
with, is kept and proved.
A yes or no about one situation is easier to get right than a whole
reading (Ryu et al., CLOVER, ICLR 2025, settle disagreements the same way).
A model is likelier to misread a sentence than to misjudge an argument,
and readings that differ show where; readings that agree show only that the
model is consistent, not that it is right.
For that,
.I prove
reads the kept reading back into English and sets each statement beside
its sentence (above): a condition turned round, a denial dropped or
added, a
.I most
made all, an atom from another sentence, found by rule from the words
and the parse, not by asking the model again.
Comparing readings,
.I prove -c -d
counts each reading's mismatches
.RB ( argue/mismatches )
and keeps, of the readings no answer rules out, the one with fewest,
and of those the one most of the others agree with: two readings
that misread a sentence alike outvote a right one, but not the words.
The logic file is left in
.IB file .logic
(or as
.B -o
names it), headed with the model's name: how a model reads the text
is the one step a rule can check only in part, so the file is there to be read,
corrected, and proved again with
.IR prove .
.IP
A long work (a chapter, an article, a thesis) is read with
.B -w
part by part, as
.I sentences -p
divides it
.RB ( -p
.IR size ,
30 sentences).
Each part is read as above, with
.BR -r :
the model is shown only that part, its sentences numbered as in the whole,
the atoms the parts before it used
.RB ( -V )
and what they concluded
.RB ( -E ),
to rest on by writing the earlier conclusion as a premise at its sentence.
A part that argues nothing (it narrates, describes, summarises) says so,
and is passed over.
Then
.I join
makes the parts one argument in steps, the model is asked, with the
instructions in
.BR /lib/phil/argue-work ,
which steps the work's main conclusion rests on and what unstated links
join them, and
.I prove
checks the whole: each part's step, the main conclusion, the links
.RB ( argue/assumed ),
and the parts' conclusions nothing after them rests on, said once
.RB ( argue/unused-steps ).
With
.B -r
as well, only the parts within that range: one chapter of a thesis.
Each part's findings are given as it is read; its files are kept in
.IB logicfile .parts ,
and
.B -j
joins them again without reading them anew, after one has been
corrected by hand.
The assumptions the joining adds are read back to the model in the
meanings their atoms declare
.RI ( prove " -e)"
and it is asked whether each says what it meant: an atom one part
declared in one sense and the joining used in another shows there, and
the model writes its lines again.
.B -c
carries on a run cut short: a part read to the end before, over the
same sentences, is not read again.
.IP
A model reads the same text differently from one run to the next.
.B -n
does the whole that many times, each afresh, into
.IB logicfile .r1 ,
.BR .r2 ,
and so on, and gives the first run's findings (its logic file is
.IR logicfile ):
one every run made as it is, one fewer made marked
.RI [in " c " of " n " runs] .
A finding is the same where its rule and its sentence are, whatever its
statement is called.
.B argue/runs-agree
is 1 if every run's verdict was the same, 0 if not: a verdict that
changes with the run rests on how the text is read.
.TP
.B phil/read
A reading: a language model, given the instructions in
.I prompt
and the question in the file
.IR question ,
writes a logic file, and only the logic file's own lines are kept, in
.IR out ;
with
.B -k
.IR n ,
.I n
readings, each by a reader of its own, in
.IB out .1
\&...
.IB out . n\fR.
A reader is blinded: it runs in a name space of its own, its directory
an empty one, an empty directory bound over
.IR out 's
directory and over
.BR /n ,
and no device attachable
.RB ( "pctl nodevs" ),
so that a reader that is a program inside InferNode cannot see the other
readings, the answers or the corpus by any name; what it would see is not
there.
A reader that is a host command
.RB ( $wwbmodel
beginning
.BR os )
runs outside InferNode's name space and is not confined by it.
Each reading's first line is
.BI # " header"
.RB ( -H ),
and its last says who read it, with which instructions (by the first
twelve digits of their SHA-1) and when, and whether it was blinded:
.EX
	# read by claude-opus-5-5, prompt /lib/phil/argue 7f3a9c1e2b4d, 2026-10-19, blinded by name space
.EE
.B -K
names atom lines to put before the reader's own.
With
.BR -c ,
the
.I checker
is given the reading and the document
.RB ( -d );
what it prints is what is wrong, and a reading it finds wrong goes to a
new reader with what is wrong, twice at most, after the text of
.B -r
(by default the question); a reading that still cannot be read is kept,
and
.I read
fails with the status
.BR unreadable .
A reading with the line
.B -z
names in it and no conclusion is taken unchecked: a reader's way of
saying there is nothing to read.
.TP
.B phil/settle
Which of several readings of a document to keep.
The prover compares them
.RB ( "phil/prove -c" )
and, where they differ, asks a question, yes or no, about a situation
that tells them apart; each goes to a model afresh, with the instructions in
.B /lib/phil/settle
and the text of
.B -p
before it, and the answers rule readings out
.RB ( -q
leaves the questions for a person).
Of those left, the one that, read back, fits the words best and that
most of the others agree with is copied over the first; what the answers
say the text takes for granted and it lacks is added to it as an
assumption.
It prints the comparison.
.TP
.B phil/senses
Whether the argument read in
.I logicfile
moves between two senses of one word.
The argument is put to a model afresh, with the instructions in
.BR /lib/phil/senses ,
the document's sentences, each statement in words, and each atom more than
one statement uses.
The model says whether each such atom is meant in one sense throughout or
in two, and if two, which statements take the second; and whether a
premise or assumption takes a word from one sense to another, a bridge
joining two atoms that use it differently.
It does not judge the argument.
The prover does: an atom in two senses is split, a second atom for the
second sense in those statements; a bridge is taken out, its formula made
one that is always true; and the argument is proved again.
Valid as read and invalid after is
.BR argue/equivocates ,
with the case in which the conclusion fails; what was proved is kept in
.IB logicfile .sense N.
Valid either way is
.BR argue/two-senses :
a shift the argument does not turn on.
It reads any reading, as
.I prove
does, and is not run by
.IR argue :
.B phil/argue -o x.logic doc; phil/senses -d doc x.logic.
The answers are kept in
.IB logicfile .senses ,
with the model, the instructions' hash and the date.
.TP
.B phil/scope
Whether the argument read in
.I logicfile
turns on the scope of a necessity or an ought: the necessity of the
consequent,
.BR "p → □q" ,
or only of the consequence,
.BR "□(p → q)" ;
for ought, narrow scope or wide.
Each premise and assumption read one way is turned over
.RB ( "prove -s" )
and the argument proved again; where it holds with the operator on the
consequent and not on the whole, or the other way about,
.B argue/scope
says so, with both readings.
Which the sentence means, and which is true, is not the prover's to
say; no model is asked.
.TP
.B phil/claims
What a document claims: every claim it asserts and every claim it gives
as another's, written down by a reader of its own
.RI ( read ,
blinded, with the instructions in
.BR /lib/phil/claims )
as a logic file with no conclusion, its premises carrying
.B says
and
.B kind
as
.I argue
writes them.
It is the reading of a source that
.I corpus
holds reports to: a reading of one argument keeps only what that
argument uses.
.B -V
gives atoms to use where they fit;
.B -r
reads only those sentences.
.TP
.B phil/says
Whom a document gives its claims to, by rule, with no model: each
sentence with a reporting verb and a named subject
.RI ( "Hobbes argues that" ),
the verb after a quotation
.RI ( "says Mill" ),
.IR "according to" ,
a name's doctrine or words,
.IR "as Bentham calls it" ,
.IR "alleged by" ,
.IR "learnt from" ;
a pronoun given to the last name reported, and
.I "it has been said"
to no one named
.RB ( - );
the writer's own
.RI ( "I hold" , " it must be said" )
left out.
Words are tagged by
.IR wwb (1)'s
tagger; a capitalized word is a name by its tag, by
.BR /lib/words ,
and not by an abstract noun's ending.
Each report is a
.B says/report
finding at its sentence, with the person and the words that say so.
.TP
.B phil/readable
What makes a logic file one the prover cannot read: a syntax error, a
statement without a sentence the document has, no conclusion, a name
twice, an atom undeclared.
With
.B -n
a file need have no conclusion, as a reading by
.I claims
has none.
Nothing printed means it can be read.
It is the checker
.I argue
gives
.IR read .
.TP
.B phil/corpus
A field's works read as one, by rule.
.I Dir
holds
.B works
(one to a line, by tabs: an id, the bibliography key, primary or
secondary, the date, the text's path, its revision, the title),
.B atoms
(the atoms the works share),
.B align
(lines
.BI same " id" : "atom shared"
and
.BI negates " id" : "atom shared" ,
with who decided), and each work's reading in
.BI readings/ id /logic\fR.
It writes each reading over the shared atoms
.RB ( readings/\fIid\fP/shared.logic :
an atom aligned
.B same
is the shared one,
.B negates
its denial, any other the work's own, named
.IB id _ atom\fR),
.B claims
(each claim, and every work that
.BR asserts ,
.BR denies ,
.BR assumes
or
.B reports
it), and
.B ids
(each statement's id, computed from its work, its kind and its formula,
so that it survives a reread).
A premise that
.B says
a work in the corpus holds a claim is checked by
.IR prove :
.B claims/contradicts-source
where what that work holds, as read, entails the claim's denial,
.B claims/misattributed
where it entails neither;
.B claims/attributed-unread
names a work not in the corpus, and
.B claims/reported-only
a reported claim no work in the corpus holds: none whose own premises,
assumptions and conclusions entail it.
Each is at the reporting work's sentence; measures follow, for
.IR wwb/gate .
.TP
.B phil/prove
Decides whether an argument's conclusion follows from its premises.
The argument is a logic file, a statement to a line, its fields
separated by tabs:
.EX
	atom	patched	the server is patched
	atom	secure	the server is secure
	P1	S1	patched -> secure
	P2	S2	secure
	A1	-	secure -> audited
	C	S3	patched
.EE
An
.B atom
line names a claim and says, in the text's words, what it stands for;
a name is letters, digits and
.LR _ ,
and not a word of the notation
.RB ( true ,
.BR T ,
.BR not ,
.BR and ,
.BR or ,
.BR implies ,
.BR iff ).
There is no equality: "other than itself" is a premise such as
.LR "all x: !moves(x, x)" .
Premises
.RB ( P1 ,
.BR P2 ,
\&...) and the conclusion
.RB ( C )
name the sentence they were taken from, numbered as
.I sentences
numbers them; an assumption
.RB ( A1 ,
\&...) is a premise the text needs but never states, and names none
.RB ( - ).
Formulas join atoms with
.B !
(not),
.B &
(and),
.B |
(or),
.B ->
(implies) and
.B <->
(if and only if), with parentheses;
.BR not ,
.BR and ,
.BR or ,
.BR implies ,
.BR iff ,
.BR && ,
.BR || ,
.B =>
and
.B <=>
are taken too, as are the logician's
¬ ∧ ∨ → ↔ ∀ ∃, since a model writes whichever it likes.
Lines beginning
.B #
are comments.
.IP
An argument may go in steps: a conclusion
.BR C1 ,
.BR C2 ,
\&... drawn from statements named in a fourth field,
.LR "from P1 P2" ,
which later steps and the argument's
.B C
may in turn rest on:
.IP
.EX
	C1	S3	caused(universe)	from P1 P2
	C	S5	created	from C1 P3
.EE
.IP
Each step is checked as a whole argument is, against what it names and
nothing else, and reported at its sentence; a conclusion with no
.B from
rests on the steps before it and the premises they do not draw on.
Steps that rest on each other are
.BR argue/cycle ,
a step nothing rests on
.BR argue/unused-step ;
.B argue/valid
is 1 if every step the conclusion depends on holds.
.IP
An objection
.RB ( O1 ,
\&...) or a reply
.RB ( R1 ,
\&...) says what it is
.LR against :
.IP
.EX
	O1	S6	some x: began(x) & !caused(x)	against P1
	R1	S7	all x: !caused(x) -> !began(x)	against O1
.EE
.IP
An attack holds if it contradicts its target, alone or given the premises no
objection disputes; one that does not is
.BR argue/misses ,
and a reply that says no more than the premise its objection disputes is
.BR argue/restates .
What survives the attacks that hold is decided by Dung's grounded semantics,
a step standing only while all it rests on stands: what falls is
.BR argue/defeated ,
with what brought it down, what attacks leave going round is
.BR argue/undecided ,
and the measure
.B argue/survives
is 1, 0 or \-1 as the conclusion stands, falls or neither.
.I map
draws the objections and replies beneath what they attack.
.IP
A premise may report another's claim rather than assert it:
.IP
.EX
	P1	S1	short	says hobbes
	P2	S2	distrust	kind empirical
.EE
.IP
A premise with
.B says
is the work's account of what the work or person named holds: never a
premise of its argument, and a step drawn from one is
.BR argue/reported-as-premise .
.B kind
says what kind of claim a statement is,
.BR empirical ,
.B normative
or
.BR conceptual .
Each is a field of its own after the formula, after
.B from
or
.B against
where there is one.
.IP
A line
.L "logic	s5"
before the statements reads the argument in the modal logic S5, with
.B []
(or
.BR □ ,
.BR nec )
for necessarily and
.B <>
(or
.BR ◇ ,
.BR pos )
for possibly;
.L "logic	kd"
in the standard deontic logic, where they are also written
.B ought
(or
.BR O )
and
.B permitted
(or
.BR P ),
which are then not atoms.
.L "logic	s5kd"
has both: necessity and possibility as in S5, and
.B ought
and
.B permitted
.RB ( O ,
.BR P )
as in the deontic logic, so that one argument can speak of what must be
and of what must be done; what is necessary holds in every world, those
as they ought to be among them, so it ought to be too.
Where necessities over every world make ever more worlds that ought to
be, those more deeply nested than the argument's own operators are left
open: an argument no case refutes even so is valid, and one refuted only
with them left open is
.BR argue/undecided .
.L "logic	k"
and
.L "logic	kt"
are the basic modal logic and its reflexive extension.
.IP
A modal word that can mean either, such as
.IR must ,
.IR may ,
.I can
or
.IR should ,
read in two senses is a fault of the text: within one reading, the
same word read as necessity at one sentence and as obligation at
another, or one sentence read both ways, is
.BR argue/equivocates ;
with
.BR -c ,
readings that give a sentence different modalities (or one none) are
compared in
.B s5kd
and the sentence is
.BR argue/modal-ambiguous ,
whatever the answers to the questions keep.
The premises hold, and the conclusion must, in the actual world; a case
that refutes the argument is told world by world.
An argument that would need more than 2048 worlds is refuted if a case
among them refutes it, and otherwise left
.BR argue/undecided ;
the dedicated modal provers
.RB ( tools/prove-bench/README )
go much further on large modal formulas.
Modal arguments are propositional: all and some are refused in them.
.B -t
writes them by the standard translation, each atom holding at a world,
for a first-order prover.
See
.B docs/ARGUMENT-FORMAT.md
in the workbench's tree.
.IP
An atom may take individuals, and a formula may say
.BI all " x" :
or
.BI some " x" :
of them:
.EX
	atom	audited(x)	x was audited
	atom	safe(x)	x is safe
	P1	S1	all x: audited(x) -> safe(x)
	P2	S2	audited(web1)
	C	S3	safe(web1)
.EE
.B all
and
.B some
range over the individuals the argument names (a
.B domain
line names more) and others, called
.BR another ,
as many as decide the argument where its logic allows
(Börger, Grädel and Gurevich,
.IR "The Classical Decision Problem" ):
none without
.B all
or
.BR some ;
.RI 2^ k
where every atom takes at most one individual and
.I k
of them take one;
one for each
.B some
where, the conclusion denied, no
.B some
lies within an
.B all
(the Bernays–Schönfinkel class).
Otherwise three are tried, and if no case refutes the argument
.I prove
looks for a proof, by Herbrand's theorem: each
.B some
in the premises and the denied conclusion is replaced by a name for what
it says exists (one that depends on the individuals of the
.B all
it lies within), and
.B all
is taken over the individuals named, those names, and the names applied
to them, a level at a time, up to four levels and sixteen individuals.
If the instances at some level contradict each other, no case of any size
refutes the argument, and it is valid
.RB ( argue/proved ,
measure
.B argue/valid
1).
Otherwise it is
.B argue/undecided
(measure
.B argue/valid
\-1), not valid: such logic cannot be decided in general, and an argument
refuted only by endlessly many individuals
.RI ( "every release has a later one" )
is undecided, not invalid.
Premises that no case tried makes true are likewise inconsistent only if
a proof shows it, and otherwise undecided.
A counterexample is the smallest found, told of the others only where the
conclusion is about them; a counterexample is always a true one, so invalid is
never in doubt.
.IP
The prover tries every case that matters and does not take anyone's word,
the model's included.
It searches the cases as a SAT solver does (clause learning, in the order of
the atoms, so that the case it shows is the plainest), and an argument of
hundreds of statements takes it well under a second;
.B tools/prove-bench
holds it to Z3, E and Vampire.
It reports, as findings, a conclusion that does not follow
.RB ( argue/invalid ),
with a case in which every premise holds and the conclusion fails,
told in the atoms' own meanings, and the name of the fallacy where it has one
(affirming the consequent, with or without
.BR all ;
denying the antecedent; a generalization from the cases named to every case);
the same case as a logician writes a model
.RB ( argue/countermodel ):
each claim's truth value, T or F, each predicate's extension over the
domain D, and in a modal logic the worlds W, what each sees (R: W × W
in S5; in s5kd □ ranges over W and O over R) and what holds at each,
made as small as it will go, as in
.LR "W = {w0, w1}; R = {(w0, w1), (w1, w1)}; w0: pay = F; w1: pay = T" ;
up to three premises of the form
.IB x " -> " y
any of which would make it follow
.RB ( argue/missing );
premises that contradict each other, from which anything follows
.RB ( argue/inconsistent );
a conclusion that only restates a premise
.RB ( argue/circular )
or is true whatever the case
.RB ( argue/trivial );
premises the conclusion does not need
.RB ( argue/unused );
a valid argument that rests on an assumption the text never states
.RB ( argue/assumed ),
or on assumptions alone, the text's own premises doing no work
.RB ( argue/begs );
a valid argument that needs a premise read from the conclusion's own
sentence, which may be the conclusion read twice
.RB ( argue/own-sentence ).
It also says what in the writing hides an argument's sense:
a premise true whatever the case
.RB ( argue/empty )
or never true
.RB ( argue/impossible );
a conclusion about something no premise mentions, and, when the argument
fails, a premise about something nothing else mentions
.RB ( argue/unlinked ),
which is where a fact was left out or one thing was named two ways,
the commonest faults in an argument a model writes down;
and one name used with different numbers of individuals
.RB ( argue/syntax ).
Then the measure
.B argue/valid
(1 or 0), for
.IR gate .
Given the document, findings about a statement are addressed at the
sentence it was taken from
.RB ( -x ,
its characters), not at the logic file's line;
a statement that cites a sentence the document does not have is
.BR argue/no-sentence .
.IP
Given the document,
.I prove
also reads each statement back into English, from its atoms' meanings,
and sets it beside its sentence, to catch the misreading a model makes
the same way every time, which no number of its readings and no answer
of its own can show:
an atom none of whose words the sentence has
.RB ( argue/unfaithful ),
the sentence perhaps not the one the statement came from;
a condition turned round, the words after
.IR if ,
.I when
or
.I every
made what follows, or
.I only if
read as
.I if
.RB ( argue/converse );
a
.IR not ,
.I never
or
.I no
the formula leaves out, or a denial in the formula of what the sentence
states plainly
.RB ( argue/negation );
and
.IR most ,
.I many
or
.I some
read as all, or
.I every
as some
.RB ( argue/quantifier );
.I either ... or
read as allowing both, or a plain
.I or
as ruling both out
.RB ( argue/or ).
Each finding gives the statement as it reads back, for a person to
judge; a paraphrase the words cannot show (a synonym, an antonym, a
pronoun) is not reported, so silence is not proof of a faithful reading.
.B -e
gives every statement's reading
.RB ( argue/says ),
whether or not it cites a sentence.
The conditions come from the parser
.RI ( parse );
without its model only the other three are looked for.
.IP
A logic file that does not parse
.RB ( argue/syntax )
is not checked, and
.I prove
fails.
.IP
With
.BR -c ,
.I prove
compares several readings of one text, logic files over the same atoms
(as
.I argue -k
makes), sentence by sentence: two readings of a sentence agree when each
entails the other, however differently written.
Where they differ
.RB ( argue/readings )
it says which files read it which way and a situation that one allows and
the other does not; where the verdict turns on the reading,
.BR argue/verdicts .
The measures
.BR argue/readings-agree ,
.BR argue/readings-differ ,
.B argue/verdicts-agree
and, for each file,
.B argue/support
(how many of its readings are the commonest) follow, and a comment names
the best supported file.
For each sentence read two ways it asks a question, as comments:
a situation the readings tell apart, every atom either uses true or false,
and whether the sentence is true in it, with the files a yes or a no
would keep:
.EX
	# question S1: Suppose "the server is secure" is true and
	#	"the server is patched" is false. Is S1 true then?
	# if yes: r1.logic r3.logic
	# if no: r2.logic
.EE
Where some readings take an assumption for granted and others do not, it
asks whether the text's argument takes it for granted, numbered
.BR U1 ,
.BR U2 ,
and so on: a yes keeps the readings that have it, stated or assumed.
.B -a
names a file of answers, lines such as
.BR "S1 no"
.RB ( C
for the conclusion,
.B U1
for an assumption,
.B M
for which sentence is the main conclusion, asked where the readings put
it at different sentences);
an answered question is
.BR argue/answered ,
and rules out the readings that answer otherwise, so the best supported
file is the best of the readings that are left.
Where the answers rule out every reading
.RB ( argue/answers-split ),
the one that agrees with most of them is kept; an assumption answered
yes that it lacks is named in a line
.BI "# assume S" n :
with its formula, the sentence of the step that rests on it, and
.I argue
adds it to the reading kept, drawn on by that step (or by the
conclusion).
.B -d
gives the document, to address findings at its sentences.
.IP
With
.BR -t ,
.I prove
writes the argument in TPTP's first-order form (fof), in ASCII as TPTP
is: premises and
assumptions as axioms, the conclusion as the conjecture, for E, Vampire
or any prover that reads it.
.B tests/prove_z3_test.sh
uses it to hold
.IR prove 's
verdicts to Z3's.
Note that
.B argue/valid
is 0 for an argument whose premises contradict each other or whose
conclusion restates a premise: such a conclusion follows in logic, but
the argument shows nothing.
.IP
A formula in a finding is shown in logic's own symbols, whatever the
file wrote:
¬ ∧ ∨ → ↔ ∀ ∃, □ and ◇ for necessity and possibility, O and P for
obligation and permission in every logic, so that it never needs the
file's logic line to be read, and brackets only where the operators'
binding needs them:
.LR "∀x (began(x) → caused(x))" ,
.LR "P abused → O limited" .
.B -A
shows them in ASCII instead, as a logic file may write them
.RB ( ! ,
.BR & ,
.BR | ,
.BR -> ,
.BR <-> ,
.BR [] ,
.BR <> ,
.BR ought ,
.BR permitted ,
.BR "all x:" ),
for a tool that cannot take UTF-8.
With
.BR -f ,
.I prove
writes the logic file out again, its formulas so shown and everything
else as it was; a file it cannot read is not written.
With
.B -s
.IR n ,
it writes it out with the
.IR n th
scope of a necessity or an ought in a premise or assumption turned over:
.B "p → □q"
as
.BR "□(p → q)" ,
and the other way, in the formula or a conjunct it asserts; a first
line, a comment, says which statement, where the reading put the
operator, and both formulas; nothing is written if the file has fewer.
.IP
With
.BR -P ,
each conclusion that follows is proved as a logician checks a proof,
by an analytic tableau (a truth tree, after Smullyan, and for the
modal logics Priest): the premises it needs set down at the actual
world w0 with the conclusion denied, each line taken apart by the rule
for its main operator and numbered with what it came from, ◇A at a
world giving A at a new world it sees
.RB ( "w0 R w1" ),
□A giving A at each world seen (in S5, and for □ and ◇ in s5kd, every
world; in KT the world itself too; for O and P a world with an
obligation is given one to see, D), ∃ a new individual and ∀ each one
on the branch; a branch closes (×) where a formula and its denial stand
at one world, and every branch closing is the proof.
The tree follows the conclusion's findings as
.B #
lines, a branch drawn as
.I map
draws one;
.B phil/tableau
typesets it.
A tree past 400 lines, 12 worlds or 8 individuals is not shown, and
says so.
.I argue
writes the logic file it keeps this way.
.IP
With
.BR -v ,
a valid conclusion is explained as an invalid one is: what it follows
from
.RB ( argue/follows ),
and, for each statement it needs, the case in which it fails without
that one
.RB ( argue/needed ).
.IP
A step that does not follow cannot stand, whatever the objections:
.B argue/survives
is 0 for an argument whose conclusion does not follow, or rests on a
step that does not.
Where a step that fails would follow from statements in the file that
its
.B from
does not name,
.B argue/missing
names them; and where one claim the premises speak of, taken as true
or false, would make a conclusion follow, it is named first, in its own
words.
.IP
With
.BR -p ,
.I prove
takes the premises and assumptions as a family of claims and puts every
subset of them to the solver, smallest first: each smallest set that
cannot all be true
.RB ( positions/impossible ,
at its first statement's sentence), and each largest set that can
.RB ( positions/coherent ,
told as what it gives up, with the plainest case in which the rest
hold), with the counts
.RB ( positions/checked ,
.BR positions/impossible ,
.BR positions/coherent ).
The conclusion, steps and objections are not used.
A line
.B rivals
.I "P10 P11"
names two rival premises (alternative accounts of the same matter, say):
a set with both is left out, not reported as impossible.
At most 16 premises and assumptions are taken; the search is
exhaustive, so its result is easy to audit, and lists the sets in the
order Python's
.B itertools.combinations
does, so it can be set beside a z3 model's.
.TP
.B phil/join
The logic files of a work's parts, as
.I argue -w
writes them, made one argument in steps: each part's statements numbered
anew, its conclusion made a step
.RB ( C1 ,
.BR C2 ,
\&...) drawn from what it was drawn from, and a premise of a later part
that is an earlier part's conclusion, at its sentence and as written
there, made that step.
.B -a
gives only the parts' atoms together,
.B -c
only their conclusions, one to a line
.RI ( "S3 formula" ),
for the next part to be given;
.B -t
.I top
adds the lines that join the parts, and where the main conclusion they give
is a part's, makes that step the main conclusion.
.TP
.B phil/map
An argument's shape, from its logic file: the conclusion, what each step is drawn
from beneath it, down to the premises, as a tree in text; steps the conclusion
does not rest on after it, and a circle where the steps go round.
Given the
.IR document ,
each statement is shown by its sentence, with its address; given
.IR prove 's
findings
.RB ( -f ),
each is marked with what
.I prove
found of it, and a statement the text never states is shown as
.B prove -e
reads it back.
.I map
decides nothing:
.IP
.EX
	phil/prove -e essay.logic essay.md > findings
	phil/map -f findings essay.logic essay.md
.EE
.IP
With
.BR -a ,
Argdown instead, for the argument-mapping tools that read it: each step a
premise-conclusion structure, its statements named as in the logic file, so that
one step's conclusion is the next one's premise and the steps are joined.
.TP
.B phil/tableau
The tableaux
.B "phil/prove -P"
writes, typeset as the textbooks set them.
Each tree is a picture
.RI ( pic )
of its branches and the lines between them, and each formula in it is
mathematics
.RI ( eqn ),
set by eqn's rules in the mathematical font: atoms and variables
italic, names of more than one letter roman, the connectives spaced as
binary operators, ¬ □ ◇ O P close to what they govern, a world's number
below its w.
A branch is a column: each line's number set right in a gutter, its
formula set left, what it came from in a column of its own; where the
tree forks, the rule that made the fork is said once, level with it; a
closed branch ends ×.
The output wants pic, then eqn, then troff; in InferNode, with the
.B infernode-troff
package bound in:
.EX
	phil/prove -P arg.logic | phil/tableau |
		pic | eqn /lib/troff/eqnrc - | troff | tr2pdf > arg.pdf
.EE
It reads what prove wrote, so a tree is set as it was proved.
.SH EXAMPLES
Whether an argument holds, at the sentences it is read from:
.IP
.EX
phil/prove agents.logic agents.md
.EE
.PP
Three readings of a passage by a model, compared, and the reading kept proved:
.IP
.EX
phil/argue -k 3 passage.md
phil/prove -v passage.md.logic passage.md
.EE
.PP
A chapter read part by part and joined:
.IP
.EX
phil/argue -w -k 3 chapter.ms
.EE
.PP
Every coherent position over a family of claims:
.IP
.EX
phil/prove -p claims.logic
.EE
